      "problem": "If $C=\\{d,i,f,e,r,n\\}$ and $D=\\{d,i,r,e,c,t\\},$ then $C\\cap D=$",
      "worked_solution": "The set $C\\cap D$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $C$ and $D.$ Since $C$ and $D$ have only the elements $d,$ $i,$ $e,$ and $r$ in common, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C\\cap D & =\\{d,i,f,e,r,n\\}\\cap \\{d,i,r,e,c,t\\} \\\\  & =\\{d,i,e,r\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
